### What does first non-repeating character mean?

The first non-repeating character is the earliest character in the string that appears exactly once. In the string `"savings"`, every letter appears once except `s` which appears twice. The first non-repeating character is `a` because it is the earliest character with a frequency of one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstNonRepeating {
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> e : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (e.getValue() == 1) return e.getKey();
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(firstUnique("savings")); // a
        System.out.println(firstUnique("aabb"));    // null
    }
}
```

```text
a
null
```

### Why use a LinkedHashMap instead of a HashMap?

A `LinkedHashMap` preserves the order in which keys were first inserted. When you iterate its entries, they come out in insertion order, which is the order characters first appeared in the string. This lets you find the first unique character directly from the map.

A plain `HashMap` does not guarantee any iteration order. If you use a `HashMap`, you would need to rescan the original string in order and look up each character's count to find the first unique one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class HashMapAlternative {
    // Using plain HashMap + string rescan
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        // Rescan the string to find the first with count 1
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (freq.get(c) == 1) return c;
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(firstUnique("rajesh")); // r (all unique, first one)
    }
}
```

```text
r
```

### How does the fixed-array approach work for a known alphabet?

If the input contains only lowercase ASCII letters, a fixed `int[26]` array is faster and uses constant space. Count frequencies in the first pass, then rescan the string and return the first character whose count is one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FixedArrayApproach {
    public static int firstUniqueIndex(String s) {
        int[] freq = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            freq[c - 'a']++;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (freq[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] == 1) return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "settlement";
        int idx = firstUniqueIndex(code);
        System.out.println("Index: " + idx + ", Char: " + (idx >= 0 ? code.charAt(idx) : "none"));
    }
}
```

```text
Index: 1, Char: e
```

### How do the three approaches compare?

| Feature | LinkedHashMap | HashMap + rescan | int[26] array |
|---|---|---|---|
| Preserves order | Yes, in the map | No, rescan string | No, rescan string |
| Space | O(k) distinct chars | O(k) distinct chars | O(1) fixed 26 slots |
| Character set | Any Unicode | Any Unicode | Lowercase ASCII only |
| Speed constant | Moderate (hashing overhead) | Moderate | Fastest (array access) |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ApproachComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "bankaccount";
        // All three produce the same answer: 'b' (first unique character)
        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap: " + linkedHashMap(input));
        System.out.println("HashMap+rescan: " + hashMapRescan(input));
        System.out.println("Array: " + fixedArray(input));
    }

    static Character linkedHashMap(String s) {
        java.util.Map<Character, Integer> m = new java.util.LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) m.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (var e : m.entrySet()) if (e.getValue() == 1) return e.getKey();
        return null;
    }

    static Character hashMapRescan(String s) {
        java.util.Map<Character, Integer> m = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) m.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) if (m.get(c) == 1) return c;
        return null;
    }

    static Character fixedArray(String s) {
        int[] f = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) f[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) if (f[c - 'a'] == 1) return c;
        return null;
    }
}
```

```text
LinkedHashMap: b
HashMap+rescan: b
Array: b
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, strings where all characters repeat, strings where all characters are unique, and single-character strings.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstUniqueEdgeCases {
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (var e : freq.entrySet()) if (e.getValue() == 1) return e.getKey();
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + firstUnique(""));       // null
        System.out.println("All repeat: " + firstUnique("aabb")); // null
        System.out.println("All unique: " + firstUnique("abc")); // a
        System.out.println("Single: " + firstUnique("z"));      // z
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: null
All repeat: null
All unique: a
Single: z
```

### What about the streaming variant?

If characters arrive one at a time and you need to answer the first unique character at any moment, a queue of candidates combined with a count map gives an efficient online algorithm. You add each character to the count map and enqueue it if it is new. Then poll from the queue, discarding characters whose count has risen above one, until the front is a true unique character.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Queue;

public class StreamingFirstUnique {
    private final Map<Character, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
    private final Queue<Character> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    public void add(char c) {
        count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        queue.add(c);
    }

    public Character firstUnique() {
        while (!queue.isEmpty() && count.get(queue.peek()) > 1) {
            queue.poll();
        }
        return queue.isEmpty() ? null : queue.peek();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StreamingFirstUnique stream = new StreamingFirstUnique();
        String codes = "aabcbd";
        for (char c : codes.toCharArray()) {
            stream.add(c);
            System.out.println("After '" + c + "': first unique = " + stream.firstUnique());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
After 'a': first unique = a
After 'a': first unique = null
After 'b': first unique = b
After 'c': first unique = b
After 'b': first unique = c
After 'd': first unique = c
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Both the LinkedHashMap and the plain HashMap approaches run in O(n) time, making two linear passes. The space is O(k) where k is the number of distinct characters, which is bounded by the alphabet size. For the fixed `int[26]` array approach, space is O(1) because the array size is constant regardless of input length.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Rajesh uses this to derive a sub-key for promotional coupon codes. From a generated code, the system picks the first letter that appears only once, which reduces collisions in a secondary index. When no character is unique, such as a code where every letter repeats, the system returns a clear sentinel so Priya's code can fall back to another key-generation strategy.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CouponSubKey {
    public static String deriveSubKey(String couponCode) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : couponCode.toCharArray()) {
            freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (var entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) {
                return String.valueOf(entry.getKey());
            }
        }
        return "FALLBACK"; // no unique character
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("SubKey: " + deriveSubKey("SAVER")); // S (all unique)
        System.out.println("SubKey: " + deriveSubKey("AABB")); // FALLBACK
    }
}
```

```text
SubKey: S
SubKey: FALLBACK
```
